    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 061957\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1257 PM MST Sun Sep 6 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Aside from some light shower activity lingering across Joshua\n  Tree National Park through this afternoon, mostly dry and quiet\n  weather conditions are expected through the first half of this \n  week. \n\n- Thunderstorm chances will start to increase, mainly across\n  central and eastern Arizona, by the latter half of the upcoming\n  week.\n \n- As high pressure builds over the region, temperatures will heat\n  up to above normal levels through the middle of the week. \n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH TUESDAY/... \nLatest water vapor satellite imagery as well as streamline \nanalysis reveal the subtropical high pressure centered over the \nsouthern Plains while an upper- level trough continues to be \nsituated across the Pacific Northwest. This upper-level trough \nwill finally begin to migrate east northeastward later today \nthrough Monday towards the Intermountain West and into south- \ncentral Canada by Tuesday while weakening in the process. As it \ndoes so, it will allow the subtropical ridge to gradually build \nwestward into our neck of the woods during the first half of the \nupcoming week.\n\nA plume of tropical moisture originating from Hurricane Marie, \nwhich continues to spin well west of the Baja Peninsula Coast, and\nan upper-level jet streak over the Great Basin Region are \nsupporting an extensive band of light to locally moderate\nrainfall, which extends from the southern CA coast east-\nnortheastward towards the Mohave Desert Region. The bulk of the \nactivity is expected to remain over these areas through this \nevening with Joshua Tree National Park expected to be the only \narea within our CWA to observe some light rainfall activity. With \nthis large cluster of rainfall, outflow winds from the northwest \nare expected to impact much of the lower deserts during the \nafternoon and evening hours, with peak gusts of 20-30 mph likely \nas depicted by the hi-res guidance. Afternoon high temperatures \nwill top out between 102-106 degrees across the south-central AZ \nlower deserts with slightly cooler readings between 98-105 degrees\nacross the western deserts due to more cloud cover. \n \nConditions will remain quiet during the first half of the week as\nthe overall lack of instability and forcing will hinder storm \nactivity. The bigger story with the building ridge of high \npressure will be the rising temperatures. Latest guidance show 500\nmb heights rising from 590-593dm on Monday to 594-597dm by early \nWednesday, which is near record strength for this time of the \nyear. As a result, temperatures will gradually heat up with the \nlatest NBM now showing lower desert highs reaching the upper 100s \nto some areas approaching 110 degrees by Tuesday and likely be \nnear similar values through the middle portion of the week. These \nhigh temperatures combined with early morning lows in the mid to \nupper 80s are likely to cause HeatRisk levels to reach the Major \nCategory and thus if these temperature trends continue, extreme \nheat products will likely be needed for much of the lower deserts \nin the subsequent forecast packages.\n\n&\n\n.LONG TERM /WEDNESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/... \nHeading into the latter half of the workweek, ensemble model \nguidance has come into slightly better agreement on the overall \nsynoptic pattern. The models have come into better agreement on \nthe placement of the features, but still differ on the overall \nstrength of the features. Both the GEFS and the EPS show a \nshortwave trough across the northern CONUS and southern Canada, a \nbroad subtropical ridge stretching across much of the lower 2/3 of\nCONUS, along with a closed low off the coast of northern \nCalifornia. The EPS continues to be the slightly stronger \nsolution, with a stronger closed low in the Pacific and a slightly\nstronger subtropical high. This overall better agreement is \nreflected with the slightly smaller temperature spread in the NBM \ninterquartile range, which has narrowed to less than 5 degrees. \nThe GEFS show the high at peak strength (H5 heights of 594-597 dm)\non Wednesday before weakening (H5 heights of 591-594 dm) by the \nlatter half of the week. The high looks to continue to weaken (H5\nheights of 588-592 dm) heading into the weekend. Therefore, \ntemperatures are forecasted to remain about 5 degrees above normal\nduring the middle of the week before beginning a slight cooling \ntrend for the end of the workweek and into the weekend closer to \nnear normal levels. \n\nAs for shower and storm chances, the ensemble members continue to \nshow those chances starting to increase, for at least portions of \nthe CWA, beginning on Wednesday. PWATs look to increase to around \n1.5-1.9\" for the latter half of the week, so moisture should not be \na limiting factor. With the high forecasted to move overhead, \nrain chances will likely be limited to the higher terrain areas \nacross eastern Arizona on Wednesday and Thursday. Rain chances \nmay extend into the lower deserts of south-central Arizona heading \ninto the weekend. However, the current WPC PoPs of 30-40%, for \nFriday into Saturday, seem rather aggressive, especially this far \nout. WPC's Saturday PoPs of 10-30% seem more reasonable.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1741Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nThe main weather concern will be gusty winds later this evening.\nWinds will maintain a westerly component throughout the period\nwith winds shifting out of the NW by 23Z-00Z where gusts will \nalso begin to ramp up. KPHX and KDVT can expect gusts between \n20-25kts between 23Z-04Z, while elsewhere may also experience \nbrief periods of gusty conditions but not enough to warrant \ninclusion in the TAF. Tomorrow morning is expected to have some \nVRB conditions but should still maintain a mostly westerly \ncomponent.\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nThick mid/high cigs with at most virga/sprinkles will persist\nthrough this evening, albeit with little to no operational impact.\nConfidence remains high that NW winds with gusts between 20-25kts\nwill develop mid/late this afternoon. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nTemperatures will be slightly below normal across SE CA today.\nOtherwise, temperatures will warm to above normal levels through\nthe middle of the week. Rain chances today will be confined to \nJoshua Tree National Park, with chances for wetting rainfall very \nlow. MinRH's will generally be around 20-30% area wide through \nmuch of this upcoming week with good overnight recoveries. Some \nelevated afternoon/early evening breeziness out of the northwest \ncan be expected this afternoon and evening with peak gusts of \n20-30 mph. A more typical diurnal wind pattern with some \noccasional afternoon upslope breeziness can be expected through \nmuch of this coming week.\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Lojero\nLONG TERM...Berislavich/Lojero\nAVIATION...Ryan \nFIRE WEATHER...Lojero/Berislavich\n",
